C = [
("h1", "2. Elijah in Kings: The Man the Promise Names"),
("p", "Claim: if Malachi 4:5 names a man, the first thing to ask is who the man was and what he did, and the book of Kings answers in five scenes. Each scene is read here as written, and each is tied to the later text that takes it up, so that a reader can see which of Elijah’s works the New Testament recalls and which it does not. The scenes also contain two links to Malachi’s own paragraph that are easy to miss: Elijah’s prayer on Carmel uses the very phrase of Malachi 4:6, and his one great journey ends at the mountain named in Malachi 4:4."),
("sub", "2.1 The shut heaven"),
("p", "Elijah enters the record without introduction. “And Elijah the Tishbite, who was of the inhabitants of Gilead, said unto Ahab, As the LORD God of Israel liveth, before whom I stand, there shall not be dew nor rain these years, but according to my word” (1 Kings 17:1). He is named, he is given a place, he stands “before” the LORD, and he sets a term on the heaven: no dew or rain “but according to my word.” The word is his only because it is first the LORD’s, for the next verse says, “And the word of the LORD came unto him, saying” (1 Kings 17:2), and the brook, the ravens and the widow of Zarephath follow at the LORD’s command (1 Kings 17:3, 4, 9)."),
("p", "Three later texts take up the shut heaven, and each gives a number or a name. The Lord says in the synagogue at Nazareth that there were many widows in Israel “in the days of Elias, when the heaven was shut up three years and six months, when great famine was throughout all the land” (Luke 4:25). James writes that Elias “prayed earnestly that it might not rain: and it rained not on the earth by the space of three years and six months” (James 5:17). The book of Kings itself says only that the word came to Elijah “in the third year” (1 Kings 18:1). James adds that “Elias was a man subject to like passions as we are” (James 5:17), which is the New Testament’s own statement that the man was a man. Gospel Commentary Volume 2 reads the Nazareth sermon as the Lord’s account of Israel’s history, and this volume cites it and does not repeat it. Revelation 11:6 speaks of “power to shut heaven, that it rain not in the days of their prophecy,” and the verse is cited here and read in chapter 11, where the figures of the witnesses are set out and not named."),
("sub", "2.2 The widow’s son"),
("p", "At Zarephath the widow’s son falls sick “and his sickness was so sore, that there was no breath left in him” (1 Kings 17:17). The woman’s first thought is of her sin, and Elijah’s is of the LORD’s name: “O LORD my God, hast thou also brought evil upon the widow with whom I sojourn, by slaying her son?” (1 Kings 17:20). He stretches himself on the child three times and prays, “let this child’s soul come into him again,” and “the LORD heard the voice of Elijah; and the soul of the child came into him again, and he revived” (1 Kings 17:21, 22). The woman’s verdict is the verdict of the scene: “Now by this I know that thou art a man of God, and that the word of the LORD in thy mouth is truth” (1 Kings 17:24)."),
("p", "The scene is the first raising of the dead recorded in the Bible, and the volume names it for one reason only. The Lord’s words at Nazareth about Elias say that he “was sent” to a widow of Sidon, “unto Sarepta, a city of Sidon” (Luke 4:26), the same Zarephath. The sending is the Lord’s own word for the errand of 1 Kings 17:9, and it will matter later that the Lord uses the verb of sending for Elijah, as Malachi does."),
("sub", "2.3 Carmel: the heart turned back"),
("p", "The third year brings the meeting with Ahab. “Art thou he that troubleth Israel?” the king asks, and Elijah answers, “I have not troubled Israel; but thou, and thy father’s house, in that ye have forsaken the commandments of the LORD, and thou hast followed Baalim” (1 Kings 18:17, 18). The contest is set on Carmel with “all Israel” and four hundred and fifty prophets of Baal (1 Kings 18:19). Elijah’s first word is to the people: “How long halt ye between two opinions? if the LORD be God, follow him: but if Baal, then follow him. And the people answered him not a word” (1 Kings 18:21). He says of himself, “I, even I only, remain a prophet of the LORD” (1 Kings 18:22)."),
("p", "Two details of the altar bear on Malachi. “And he repaired the altar of the LORD that was broken down. And Elijah took twelve stones, according to the number of the tribes of the sons of Jacob, unto whom the word of the LORD came, saying, Israel shall be thy name” (1 Kings 18:30, 31). The altar is Israel’s altar and is rebuilt for all twelve tribes, in a day when the kingdom was divided. It is the work of one man at an altar for the whole people. And at the evening sacrifice he prays: “Hear me, O LORD, hear me, that this people may know that thou art the LORD God, and that thou hast turned their heart back again” (1 Kings 18:37). The fire falls, the people fall on their faces, and they say, “The LORD, he is the God; the LORD, he is the God” (1 Kings 18:38, 39)."),
("p", "The phrase in the prayer is “turned their heart back again.” Malachi’s is “turn the heart of the fathers to the children, and the heart of the children to their fathers” (Malachi 4:6). The English words are the same word, to turn the heart, and the direction in Kings is toward the LORD, as Luke 1:16 says of John: “And many of the children of Israel shall he turn to the Lord their God.” The volume does not say that Malachi 4:6 is only the Carmel work repeated. It says that the one scene in which the Bible shows Elijah turning a whole people’s heart is a scene in which he uses Malachi’s phrase, and that a reader who asks what the promised prophet’s work will look like has the answer in Kings."),
("sub", "2.4 Horeb"),
("p", "After Carmel, Jezebel’s threat sends Elijah south to Beer-sheba and into the wilderness, where he sits under a juniper tree and asks to die (1 Kings 19:2–4). An angel feeds him twice and he goes “in the strength of that meat forty days and forty nights unto Horeb the mount of God” (1 Kings 19:8). He lodges in a cave, and the LORD asks the question twice: “What doest thou here, Elijah?” (1 Kings 19:9, 13). The answer is the same both times: “I have been very jealous for the LORD God of hosts: because the children of Israel have forsaken thy covenant, thrown down thine altars, and slain thy prophets with the sword; and I, even I only, am left; and they seek my life, to take it away” (1 Kings 19:14)."),
("p", "Horeb is the mountain named in Malachi 4:4: “the law of Moses my servant, which I commanded unto him in Horeb for all Israel.” Elijah is the one prophet whose recorded journey ends at that mountain, and he goes there to say that Israel has forsaken the covenant given there. The wind, the earthquake and the fire pass, and the LORD is not in them, “and after the fire a still small voice” (1 Kings 19:11, 12). Then the LORD commissions him: Hazael, Jehu and Elisha, and an answer to his loneliness: “Yet I have left me seven thousand in Israel, all the knees which have not bowed unto Baal, and every mouth which hath not kissed him” (1 Kings 19:15–18). Elijah’s own report is that all Israel is against him; the LORD’s is that a seven thousand remain. The volume notes the contrast and leaves the seven thousand to the places that read them."),
("p", "Elisha’s call comes in the same chapter: Elijah “passed by him, and cast his mantle upon him,” and Elisha “arose, and went after Elijah, and ministered unto him” (1 Kings 19:19, 21). The mantle, which falls again in 2 Kings 2, is the first sign that the prophet’s work can pass to another."),
("sub", "2.5 Naboth’s vineyard"),
("p", "The last scene in 1 Kings is a word of judgment. Ahab has taken Naboth’s vineyard, and the LORD sends Elijah: “Hast thou killed, and also taken possession?” (1 Kings 21:19). Ahab answers, “Hast thou found me, O mine enemy?” and the prophet says, “I have found thee: because thou hast sold thyself to work evil in the sight of the LORD” (1 Kings 21:20). The sentence on the house is set out. Then comes the moment that matters for the volume: “And it came to pass, when Ahab heard those words, that he rent his clothes, and put sackcloth upon his flesh, and fasted” (1 Kings 21:27). The LORD says to Elijah, “Seest thou how Ahab humbleth himself before me? because he humbleth himself before me, I will not bring the evil in his days: but in his son’s days will I bring the evil upon his house” (1 Kings 21:29)."),
("p", "The scene shows what the first volume of this series reads as the conditional word of a prophet. A judgment spoken by Elijah is delayed when the man judged humbles himself. The volume draws one point from it for the question of Malachi’s Elijah. The prophet who is to come before the day is a prophet whose word may be answered, and the LORD’s answer to a turned heart is mercy for a time."),
("sub", "2.6 Ahaziah and the fire"),
("p", "The book of Kings closes its first volume with Ahaziah, who falls through a lattice and sends to Baal-zebub of Ekron. The angel of the LORD tells Elijah to meet the messengers: “Is it not because there is not a God in Israel, that ye go to enquire of Baal-zebub the god of Ekron?” (2 Kings 1:3). The king asks what manner of man met them, and they answer, “He was an hairy man, and girt with a girdle of leather about his loins. And he said, It is Elijah the Tishbite” (2 Kings 1:8). The text does not say whether the “hairy” is the man’s or his garment’s. Mark’s description of John is that he “was clothed with camel’s hair, and with a girdle of a skin about his loins” (Mark 1:6), and the likeness is in the wording and is noted without being pressed."),
("p", "Fire falls twice on captains of fifty, “If I be a man of God, then let fire come down from heaven, and consume thee and thy fifty” (2 Kings 1:10, 12), and the third captain falls on his knees and begs, “let my life now be precious in thy sight,” and the angel says, “Go down with him: be not afraid of him” (2 Kings 1:13, 15). The disciples will recall the fire when a Samaritan village refuses the Lord, and their words, “wilt thou that we command fire to come down from heaven, and consume them, even as Elias did?” (Luke 9:54), are the New Testament’s only direct reference to this scene. The Lord’s answer is read in chapter 10."),
("ctbl", "Elijah’s works in Kings and the later texts that take them up.", [
    ("Scene", "What the text says", "Later text"),
    ("The shut heaven (1 Kings 17:1; 18:1)", "No dew or rain but at his word; the third year", "Luke 4:25; James 5:17, 18; Revelation 11:6 (cited)"),
    ("The widow of Zarephath (1 Kings 17:8–24)", "Sent to a widow of Sidon; her son revived", "Luke 4:26"),
    ("Carmel (1 Kings 18)", "Twelve stones; fire; “turned their heart back again”", "Malachi 4:6; Luke 1:16"),
    ("Horeb (1 Kings 19)", "Forty days to the mount of God; “I, even I only, am left”; the seven thousand", "Malachi 4:4"),
    ("Naboth (1 Kings 21)", "A judgment delayed when the king humbles himself", "The conditional word"),
    ("Ahaziah (2 Kings 1)", "A hairy man; fire from heaven on captains of fifty", "Mark 1:6; Luke 9:54"),
  ], [0.28, 0.4, 0.32]),
("sub", "2.7 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Kings gives Elijah a name, a place, a title and a life, and the New Testament speaks of him as a man subject to like passions as we are.",
    "On Carmel he prays that the people may know the LORD “hast turned their heart back again,” which is the work Malachi 4:6 names.",
    "Elijah’s one long journey ends at Horeb, the mountain named in Malachi 4:4.",
    "A word of judgment spoken by Elijah was delayed when Ahab humbled himself, and the prophet’s work is shown as aimed at turning.",
    "The fire of 2 Kings 1 is the one act of Elijah the disciples name in the Gospels as an example to follow, and the Lord answers them; the answer is read in chapter 10.",
  ]),
]
